Description: avfilter/tonemap_cuda: keep a sample on a pivot in the piece that starts there
 The reshape kernel reads a 10-bit sample from a 16-bit texture and
 rescales it in float32. For some codes, 545 among them, the result is
 one ulp below the pivot of the same code, which is stored as
 code * (1.0f / 1023). A sample on such a pivot took the piece before.
 tonemapx divides each sample by 1023 and is not affected.
 .
 Store each inner pivot 2^-21 lower. That is 8 ulps at 0.5, above the
 rounding of the read, and far below the 1/1023 between two 10-bit
 codes. The texture filters chroma between texels. On the NVIDIA GPU
 these patches were tested on, every filtered read is a whole 16-bit
 step. One step is 1/64 of a 10-bit code and 32 times the margin. So a
 filtered read below a pivot code lies at least one step below it, and
 the margin moves no filtered chroma into the piece above. A GPU that
 filtered to finer steps could move chroma filtered to within 2^-21
 below a pivot.
 .
 On a synthetic profile 5 picture with patches on four chroma pivots,
 all 12 patches on a pivot took the piece below before this patch, up
 to 16 chroma codes off, and none after. On that picture only samples
 that read exactly a pivot code change.
 .
 Other synthetic frames hold random chroma codes around a P pivot at
 code 546 and a T pivot at code 550. They are cropped to odd sizes from
 511 by 511 to 1919 by 1079. Codes 546 and 550 read at or above their
 own pivot without a margin, so only a filtered read could take another
 piece. The filter wrote the same output for these frames with this
 margin as with none. A margin of 2^-15, two steps, changed about 0.5 %
 of their samples.
